{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Giorgio Gallo, Michael D. Grigoriadis, Robert E. Tarjan","relevance":"Proof-technology candidate for a parametric-cut or active-set algorithm for the CL-049 reduced LP; useful if positive-pressure branches can be encoded as a monotone min-cut sweep.","title":"A Fast Parametric Maximum Flow Algorithm and Applications","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/0218003","venue_year":"SIAM Journal on Computing, 1989"},{"authors":"Jean-Claude Picard, Maurice Queyranne","relevance":"Proof-technology candidate for representing lattices of optimal cuts/closures if the zero-pressure feasibility or active-set conditions reduce to max-flow/min-cut certificates.","title":"On the structure of all minimum cuts in a network and applications","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01581031","venue_year":"Mathematical Programming, 1982"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The lower bound may be correct but far from tight when segment capacities bind; duplicates or merely nondecreasing lows may introduce boundary cases that obscure the clean tail-weight formula.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","counterexample search"],"summary":"Audit and sharpen the solver's increasing-low zero-pressure lemma. Prove carefully that the proposed dual tail weights have nonnegative alpha_j, sum alpha_j + beta = 1, and psi_i = 0 for strictly increasing lows. Then determine whether Z_inc is exact under natural capacity assumptions, and record the minimal counterexample when it is not.","title":"Increasing-Low Zero-Pressure Theorem","why_promising":"It directly extends the accepted two-low p<q non-capacity branches and explains the three-low 291/100 certificate; it is the cleanest new structure in the solver attempt."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The weighted final-debt constraint may prevent a simple Hall theorem, or the resulting cut inequalities may be no simpler than the original LP.","suggested_lenses":["algorithmic proof","certificate extraction"],"summary":"Turn the solver's exactness certificate for a fixed active k into a max-flow or Hall-type feasibility theorem. Model segment capacities B_i as supply, allowed masses x_{i,j} only on j >= max(k,i+1), low-coverage constraints as nested demands S_t - Z_inc, and final high debt as a weighted budget. Derive min-cut inequalities whose failure identifies the first capacity obstruction.","title":"Flow Certificate For Exactness","why_promising":"It would convert a candidate primal certificate into necessary and sufficient finite inequalities, giving explorers a precise way to prove exactness or produce capacity-saturation counterexamples."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The max over quote levels in psi_i can make the active-set geometry piecewise-linear but messy; this may collapse to generic LP solving without a readable theorem.","suggested_lenses":["active-set algorithm","duality"],"summary":"Use CL-049 complementary slackness to design a certificate-producing active-set algorithm. Start from the zero-pressure pool; when the flow/Hall certificate fails, identify the violated cut, declare the corresponding segment pressures psi_i positive, and solve the smaller residual problem. Compare the first nontrivial residual with the two-low p<q third branch S_1 - pB_0.","title":"Positive-Pressure Active Sets","why_promising":"The two-low closed form already shows how capacity saturation changes the formula; a recursive active-set rule could be the real pooling/PAVA analogue the run has been searching for."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Running maxima m_{i,j} may create nonlocal constraints that defeat any record-only or adjacent-merge decomposition; small counterexamples may be abundant.","suggested_lenses":["finite LP regression","structural decomposition"],"summary":"Investigate nonmonotone one-drought lows by comparing the CL-049 LP to record-based reductions. Test sequences such as 0.4,0.9,0.5; 0.9,0.4,0.8; duplicate lows; and zero-gap segments. Ask whether low constraints split into increasing record pools, whether subrecord lows are dominated, or whether they create genuinely new active constraints.","title":"Nonmonotone Low Structure","why_promising":"The solver's lemma uses strict monotonicity exactly where psi_i vanishes. Understanding the first nonmonotone failures is essential before proposing any general one-drought pooling rule."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Repeated droughts may require many state variables, and normalized D_n can be diluted in ways that make local formulas hard to compose into a global gluing theorem.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","separator search"],"summary":"Extend the one-drought debt viewpoint to two or more droughts sharing the same high h. Formulate the full finite-prefix LP with explicit high-debt variables carried across droughts, then test whether local one-drought values add, share debt, or cancel. Search for a path with D_n = o(n) but no fixed sublinear envelope, or for a debt recursion proving the opposite in two-level paths.","title":"Repeated-Drought Debt Ledger","why_promising":"This attacks the main unresolved D_n=o(n) versus anytime-gluing problem using the strongest local machinery now available, rather than raw delay tails."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Experiments can suggest false closed forms unless every output includes matching rational primal and dual certificates; full D_n LPs may grow quickly.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","formal verification"],"summary":"Build an exact rational regression suite for the formal left-limit finite-prefix LP and for the CL-049 reduced LP. For each candidate path, output D_n, primal masses, dual weights, psi_i, active constraints, and certificate residuals. Prioritize capacity-saturated increasing lows, nonmonotone lows, M>1, duplicate lows, and repeated-drought prefixes.","title":"Rational LP Regression Harness","why_promising":"The run already had a false 14/45 value before certificates caught it. A small exact harness would make explorer work faster and make future conjectures critic-friendly."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
